  <div class="section-label">The State Environmental Study Process</div>
  <h2>State Environmental Study: Current Status</h2>

  <p>The transition of SR-177 into Weber County is managed through a State Environmental Study — an analytical process that evaluates the physical, biological, and social impacts of the proposed extension. UDOT completed scoping (Fall 2025 – Early 2026) and alternatives development (Early 2026 – Summer 2026), and published the Draft State Environmental Study on November 6, 2026.{% cite "udot-ses-schedule" %}{% cite "udot-draft-ses-vol1-nov2026" %}</p>

  <p><strong>How UDOT chose the alignment.</strong> Building on the 2001 and 2009 North Legacy studies, which both recommended the 5100 West corridor, the study team compared three concepts along 5100 West: an east shift, a west shift, and a "hybrid" that runs west of 5100 West in the south and crosses to the east side after the 5500 South interchange. The hybrid had the fewest relocations (84 at the concept stage, versus 89 and 91), the smallest aquatic and agricultural-protection-area impacts, and used 33 parcels UDOT already owns, so it became the Build Alternative.{% cite "udot-draft-ses-appendix-nov2026" %} The draft then identifies that Build Alternative as the Preferred Alternative "because it meets the purpose and need for the project."{% cite "udot-draft-ses-vol1-nov2026" %}</p>

  <p><strong>What the Preferred Alternative includes.</strong> A new four-lane freeway about 3.5 miles long; diamond interchanges at 1800 North and 5500 South; a single-point urban interchange at 4000 South; 5100 West kept as a frontage road between about 6000 South and 5500 South and between 5100 South and 4000 South; 5300 West extended from 5100 South to 5900 South; underpasses keeping 2425 North and 5100 South open; several side streets ending in cul-de-sacs near 5100 West; and a 12-foot shared-use path on the west side with grade-separated crossings of 1800 North, 5500 South, and 4000 South and an underpass for the planned Hooper Slough Trail.{% cite "udot-draft-ses-vol1-nov2026" %}</p>

  <p>The May 2026 public ArcGIS working layers labeled <code>21158 Hybrid Concept Design</code> share the study's project number and the "hybrid" name, and appear to show that concept; the draft study's Volume 2 mapbooks are the controlling source.{% cite "arcgis-sr177-project-layers-may2026" %}{% cite "udot-draft-ses-vol2-nov2026" %}</p>

  <div class="callout warning">
    <p><strong>Important caveat:</strong> The Preferred Alternative is a draft. UDOT is taking comments through December 11, 2026, will respond to them in the Final State Environmental Study (expected early 2027), and says property impacts may be refined during final design and right-of-way acquisition.{% cite "udot-ses-schedule" %}{% cite "udot-draft-ses-vol1-nov2026" %}</p>
  </div>

